{0 Footnotes}

Treat footnote labels as first-class anchors within a document: complete them,
jump between reference and definition, list every reference, rename a label, and
report the two ways a footnote can be wrong.

{v
Kantorovich relaxed the problem[^kant], and the relaxation is tight[^kant].

[^kant]: Kantorovich, 1942.
v}

A footnote label is an identifier with a definition and a set of references —
the same shape as a heading slug or a block id, which the server already
handles — but it is {e document-scoped}: [\[^kant\]] in one note has nothing to
do with [\[^kant\]] in another. Every rule below follows from that.

{1 Parsing}

Footnotes are a Cmarkit extension, enabled by [~strict:false], which
{!Oystermark.Parse.of_string} already passes — there is no separate footnote
knob, and no parser change is needed. The AST carries:

{ul
  {- {b definitions} as [Cmarkit.Block.Ext_footnote_definition] nodes, whose
     label is [Cmarkit.Block.Footnote.label] and whose content is
     [Cmarkit.Block.Footnote.block]. A label defined twice yields {e two}
     nodes, so every occurrence of a duplicate is reachable;}
  {- {b defined references} as inline links satisfying
     [Cmarkit.Inline.is_footnote_reference], i.e. a label key starting with
     [^].}
}

{b An undefined reference is not in the AST.} A footnote reference is a
CommonMark shortcut reference link, and a shortcut reference with no matching
definition is not a link at all: [\[^ghost\]] survives parsing as ordinary
[Cmarkit.Inline.Text]. The two features that exist for undefined labels — the
[undefined footnote] diagnostic and the undefined-label completion item —
therefore cannot be written as AST walks, and a collector that only walked the
AST would report nothing in exactly the case worth reporting.

So {b references are collected textually}, not from the AST: a scan of the
source for [\[^label\]] outside code spans and code blocks, which finds
defined and undefined references alike and by one rule rather than two. The
AST is used for definitions, where it is authoritative about extent — a
footnote body runs to a dedent, which no textual rule reconstructs.

Definitions carry [Cmarkit.Meta.textloc] under [~locs:true]; textual
references carry the byte range the scan found them at. A shared collector —
the footnote analogue of {!Lsp_lib.Link_collect} — returns both for a
document, and the features below are views over it.

{2:scan What the scan owes the parser}

This scan is the one place in the server where a {e textual} search decides
what exists, rather than where something already known to exist is written
down.

The difference is worth naming, because it changes what a mistake costs.
Elsewhere — {!Lsp_lib.Rename} finding a definition line, say — the entity comes from the AST or
the vault index, and the line search only re-locates it. A miss there points at
the wrong line for something that genuinely exists. Here the scan {e is} the
source of truth for references, so a false positive invents a footnote
reference: the [undefined footnote] diagnostic then warns about it, and
{!page-"feature-rename"} rewrites it.

So the scan has to decline what the parser would have declined for us. A
[\[^x\]] is {b not} a reference when it is:

{t
  | Context | Example |
  |---------|---------|
  | in a code span | [`see [^x] here`] |
  | in a fenced or indented code block | a line inside [```] fences |
  | escaped | [\\\[^x\]] |
  | inside wikilink brackets | [\[\[note#^x\]\]], which is a block id |
  | the definition's own label | [\[^x\]: body] — a definition, not a reference to itself |
}

None of these need judgement about {e footnotes}; they are all "is this
position ordinary prose", which the parser already knows. Where the two
disagree, the parser is right. An implementation may therefore prefer to walk
the AST for {e text} nodes and scan only inside those — code, escapes and
wikilinks are excluded by construction, and only the definition line then needs
special-casing — rather than scanning raw source and re-deriving all of it.

This is the part of the feature to build first and test hardest. Every other
section here is a view over what the collector returns, so a collector that
over-reports makes five features wrong at once, and it does so in the direction
that edits the user's file.

{b Label identity is the key, not the text.} Cmarkit's
[case_sensitive_labels] defaults to [false] (CommonMark behavior) and
{!Oystermark.Parse.of_string} does not override it, so [\[^KANT\]] resolves
the definition [\[^kant\]]: [Cmarkit.Label.key] case-folds. Every rule below
that says "the same label" means the same key. Completion offers one item per
key; find-references from [\[^kant\]] includes [\[^KANT\]]; renaming
rewrites both, and both come out spelled the new way. This is the parser's
semantics, not a choice available here — a case-sensitive reading would call
a reference undefined that renders perfectly well.

{b Namespace.} Footnote labels do {e not} join the fragment namespace shared by
heading slugs, caret block ids, and attribute ids
({!page-"feature-diagnostics".duplicate_ids}). They are addressed by a
different syntax, they are not link targets, and a note may legitimately have
both a footnote [\[^kant\]] and a block id [^kant].

{1 Completion}

{2 Trigger}

Completion activates when the cursor sits inside an unclosed [\[^] on the
current line: the text between it and the cursor is the prefix. The
[completionProvider] trigger characters — [\[], [#] and [(] — gain [^].

{b Precedence.} This is the third detector in {!Lsp_lib.Completion}, and it is
tried last. Wikilink detection runs first, so [\[\[note#^<cursor>\]\]] is
block-id fragment completion ({!page-"feature-completion".fragment_completion})
and not this. The two are distinguished by whether an enclosing [\[\[] was
found, not by the [^] — a caret means "block id" inside wikilink brackets and
"footnote" outside them, and nothing else disambiguates them. Markdown
destination detection ({!page-"feature-completion-markdown-links"}) runs
second and cannot collide: a destination begins after [\](], where a [\[^]
cannot be open.

A [\[^] that begins a line and is followed by [\]:] is a {e definition}, not a
reference. Completing there would suggest the label being defined; no
completions are offered on a definition line.

{2 Items}

One item per definition {e key} in the current document (see the label
identity rule above): two definitions differing only in case are one item,
labelled as the first of them is spelled.

{t
  | Field      | Value |
  |------------|-------|
  | label      | the label including [^], e.g. [^kant] |
  | detail     | the first line of the footnote's content, truncated to 60 characters with an ellipsis |
  | filterText | the label without [^] |
  | textEdit   | the label without [^] — the [^] is already typed — replacing the prefix after it |
  | kind       | [CompletionItemKind.Reference] |
}

Like every other mode of {!Lsp_lib.Completion}, the item carries a [textEdit]
with an explicit range rather than an [insertText] the client places itself;
see {!page-"feature-completion-markdown-links".replace_range}. The range runs
from just after the [^] to the cursor.

[detail] is what makes this worth having: a document with [\[^1\]] through
[\[^9\]] is exactly the document where the label does not tell you which
footnote you want.

Order is by first appearance in the document — reading order, which for
numbered footnotes is also numeric order, and which ["1", "10", "2"]
alphabetical order would scramble.

An {e undefined} label already referenced elsewhere in the document is also
offered, with [detail] ["undefined"]. Referring twice to a footnote you have
not written yet is an ordinary intermediate state, and completing the second
reference keeps the two spellings identical. These labels come from the
textual scan, which is the only thing that sees them at all.

{1 Find references}

{!page-"feature-find-references"} gains a footnote activation mode, in addition
to its link and heading/block-id modes.

{ul
  {- {b Cursor on a footnote reference}: every reference to that label in this
     document, the cursor's own included, plus the definition when one exists.}
  {- {b Cursor on a footnote definition label}: every reference to it.}
}

Results are LSP [Location]s in the current document only, each covering the
full byte range of the reference (brackets included) or of the definition
label. They are sorted by range start.

Including the definition among a reference's results is a deliberate departure
from the link mode, which returns referrers only: a footnote has exactly one
definition and it is the thing you most want to reach. Reaching it is also what
{!go_to_definition} is for, and both being available is not a conflict.

{1:go_to_definition Go to definition}

{!page-"feature-go-to-definition"} gains one case: a footnote reference
navigates to its definition in the same document. An undefined label navigates
nowhere and is already diagnosed.

The reverse is not go-to-definition: on a definition, [textDocument/references]
is the request that answers "where is this used".

{1 Diagnostics}

{!page-"feature-diagnostics"} gains two document-scoped checks, both at
severity [Warning] with source ["oystermark"]:

{t
  | Message | Condition | Range |
  |---------|-----------|-------|
  | [undefined footnote: ^kant] | a reference whose label has no definition | the reference |
  | [duplicate footnote definition: ^kant] | a label defined more than once | every definition of it |
}

Every occurrence of a duplicate is reported, matching the treatment of
duplicate anchor ids.

An {e unused} definition — defined, never referenced — is {b not} reported. It
renders as an unreferenced note at the foot of the document, which is a
stylistic matter, and warning about it would fire constantly while a document
is being written.

{1 Rename}

{!page-"feature-rename"} gains footnote labels: renaming from a reference or a
definition rewrites the definition and every reference in the document, and
nothing outside it.

The new name is validated as a label: non-empty, and free of [\[], [\]], and
whitespace. An invalid new name fails the rename with a message rather than
producing a document whose footnotes have silently stopped parsing.

A new name whose {e key} is already a footnote label in the document also
fails. Renaming [\[^a\]] to [\[^b\]] where [\[^b\]] exists would merge two
footnotes into one — silently, and with one body lost — which no rename should
be able to do by accident. Renaming a label to a different spelling of itself
([\[^kant\]] to [\[^Kant\]]) is not a collision: it is the same key, and the
rename is a re-spelling.

{1 Testing}

{ul
  {- Collection: an expect test over a document with a definition, two
     references, a multi-paragraph footnote body, a label reused as a block id,
     an {b undefined} reference, and a reference {b differing in case},
     asserting the collected ranges and keys. The undefined and the miscased
     reference are the two the AST alone would get wrong, so they are the two
     the test exists for.}
  {- Scan restraint ({!scan}): a document carrying a [\[^x\]] in a code span, in
     a fenced block, escaped, and inside wikilink brackets, asserting that
     {e none} of them is collected — the failure that would put a warning on
     prose and let rename edit a code sample.}
  {- Trigger precedence: a table over cursor positions covering
     [\[^<cursor>\]], [\[\[note#^<cursor>\]\]], a definition line, and a [^]
     in prose.}
  {- Completion: offered set and ordering for a document with numeric labels
     ordered [1], [10], [2] in the source, plus the undefined-label case.}
  {- References: from a reference, from the definition, and with the cursor on
     a footnote in a document that has two.}
  {- Diagnostics: undefined, duplicate (all occurrences), and unused (no
     diagnostic).}
  {- Rename: the collision refusal, and {b property} — for a generated valid
     label, renaming and re-parsing yields a document whose footnote structure
     is isomorphic to the original, with every occurrence of the old label
     replaced whatever its case.}
}

{1 Limitations}

{ul
  {- {b Document-scoped throughout}. No vault-wide footnote index; a label is
     never resolved across files. This is the semantics, not a shortcut.}
  {- {b Inline footnotes} are not supported — Cmarkit's extension has
     definitions only.}
  {- {b No definition-creating quick fix} for an undefined label. It is a
     natural companion to
     {!page-"feature-codeaction-create-unresolved-link"} and is deliberately
     left out of this spec, which is already five features wide.}
  {- {b Not ordered.} Nothing renumbers footnotes or checks that numeric
     labels appear in order; the renderer assigns display numbers.}
  {- Positions and edits follow {!page-"feature-utf16-positions"}.}
}
